saveOutputs: false
cells:
  - kind: 1
    value: "# 11g · Step 6 – How does Jonas get there? (Spatial: pick-up, drive time, plan B)\n\nJonas cannot walk back to the village. He calls a friend in Nuremberg, who drives out to pick him up. Where should they meet, when does the pick-up happen, how long is the drive to the orthopaedics – and what if there were no appointment today?\n\n**Question:** Where can a car pick Jonas up, when does the pick-up happen, how long is the drive – and what if the practice had no appointment?\n\n- **Features:** Spatial (`SDO_NN` with `sdo_batch_size`, `SDO_ANYINTERACT`, `SDO_GEOM.RELATE`), `MATCH_RECOGNIZE` on a second stream, routing (`SDO_GCDR.ELOC_ROUTE`, GeoJSON → `SDO_GEOMETRY`), JSON filter on OSM tags\n- **Pattern:** M4 (geofence event), M2 (drive time), M3 – search by location, filter by attribute (plan B)\n- **Data:** `PARKING_LOTS`, `ZONES` (geofences), `CAR_STREAM` (the friend's car), `WATCH_STREAM`, `PROVIDERS`, `OSM_HOSPITALS` (37 hospitals from OpenStreetMap, tags as JSON)\n- **Run:** as the demo user (DOAG2026) in SQL Developer for VS Code, after `01_run_all.sql`; routing needs `SDO_GCDR.ELOC_GRANT_ACCESS` for the demo user (see `00_create_user.sql`). Run the cells from top to bottom.\n\n> Fictitious demo: no medical device and no medical advice. Places from OpenStreetMap (© OpenStreetMap contributors, ODbL); all persons and story places are synthetic."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 1
    value: "### Session settings\n\nThe story takes place on Monday, 16 November 2026, in Franconia. With the session time zone `Europe/Berlin`, all timestamps and literals read in local time.\n\nThe SQLcl settings in the second cell make the text output readable: columns as wide as their content instead of their declared length (`SQLFORMAT ansiconsole`; values from JSON are `VARCHAR2(4000)`), one header per result (`PAGESIZE`), wider lines (`LINESIZE`) and complete JSON documents and CLOBs (`LONG`, `LONGCHUNKSIZE`)."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "ALTER SESSION SET TIME_ZONE = 'Europe/Berlin';"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: "SET SQLFORMAT ansiconsole\nSET PAGESIZE 1000\nSET LINESIZE 250\nSET LONG 1000000\nSET LONGCHUNKSIZE 250"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 1. Which car parks are close to Jonas?\n\nJonas waits near the place where he sent his report.\n\n**Question:** Which car parks are close to him?\n\n**How the query answers it:** Starting point is the location of Jonas' app report (`SR-2026-11-16-0001`, 11:17). `SDO_NN` with `sdo_num_res=5` returns the five nearest car parks, `SDO_NN_DISTANCE(1)` the distance in metres.\n\n**Result:** *Wanderparkplatz Hohe Leite* (`PK03`) is 218 m away, *Parkplatz Burgblick* (`PK05`) 318 m. *Parkplatz Felsenkanzel* (`PK02`, 655 m) has `CAR_ACCESS` = false and capacity 0: it cannot be reached by car. The car park in the village centre (`PK01`) is 856 m away, *Talauenweg* (`PK04`) 2.6 km. Nearest is not enough – the car must be able to get there."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "SELECT p.parking_id,\n       p.name,\n       p.car_access,\n       p.capacity,\n       ROUND(SDO_NN_DISTANCE(1)) AS distance_m\nFROM   parking_lots p\nWHERE  SDO_NN(p.geom,\n              (SELECT s.geom FROM symptom_reports s WHERE s.report_id = 'SR-2026-11-16-0001'),\n              'sdo_num_res=5 unit=M', 1) = 'TRUE'\nORDER  BY distance_m;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 2. The nearest car park that a car can reach\n\nClose is not enough – the friend comes by car.\n\n**Question:** Which is the nearest car park a car can reach?\n\n**How the query answers it:** With an additional filter, `sdo_num_res` would count the filtered rows too. `sdo_batch_size` lets the index deliver neighbours in batches until the filter `car_access = TRUE` is met; `FETCH FIRST 1 ROW ONLY` stops after the first one.\n\n**Result:** *Wanderparkplatz Hohe Leite* (`PK03`) – the pick-up point."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "SELECT p.parking_id,\n       p.name,\n       p.capacity,\n       ROUND(SDO_NN_DISTANCE(1)) AS distance_m\nFROM   parking_lots p\nWHERE  SDO_NN(p.geom,\n              (SELECT s.geom FROM symptom_reports s WHERE s.report_id = 'SR-2026-11-16-0001'),\n              'sdo_batch_size=10 unit=M', 1) = 'TRUE'\nAND    p.car_access = TRUE\nORDER  BY distance_m\nFETCH  FIRST 1 ROW ONLY;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 3. Geofence events from two streams\n\nTwo streams converge on the car park: the friend's car and Jonas' watch.\n\n**Question:** When does each of them arrive, and when is the pick-up?\n\n**How the query answers it:** Each car park has a geofence, a 60 m × 60 m square in `ZONES`. `SDO_ANYINTERACT` in the join condition finds all points of the friend's car (`CAR_STREAM`) and of Jonas' watch (`WATCH_STREAM`) inside a geofence; the earliest point per geofence is the entry event. For Jonas only the points after his app report (11:17:10) count – in the morning he started the hike at a car park, and that is not a pick-up. The later of both entries is the pick-up; the first geofence where both arrive is the pick-up point.\n\n**Result:** the car enters the geofence of *Wanderparkplatz Hohe Leite* at 12:51:30, Jonas reaches it limping at 12:54:20 – pick-up at 12:54:20. Without the time condition the query would also report the car park in the village centre: Jonas was there at 10:05, at the start of the hike, and the car passes it at 12:50:10 – two entries almost three hours apart, which are no meeting."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "WITH car AS (\n  SELECT z.zone_id,\n         MIN(c.event_ts) AS car_enters\n  FROM   car_stream c\n  JOIN   zones z ON SDO_ANYINTERACT(c.geom, z.geom) = 'TRUE'\n  WHERE  z.zone_type = 'Geofence Parkplatz'\n  GROUP  BY z.zone_id\n),\nwalker AS (\n  SELECT z.zone_id,\n         MIN(w.event_ts) AS jonas_enters\n  FROM   watch_stream w\n  JOIN   zones z ON SDO_ANYINTERACT(w.geom, z.geom) = 'TRUE'\n  WHERE  z.zone_type = 'Geofence Parkplatz'\n  AND    w.event_ts  >= TIMESTAMP '2026-11-16 11:17:10 Europe/Berlin'   -- after the app report\n  GROUP  BY z.zone_id\n)\nSELECT zone_id,\n       z.name                                          AS geofence,\n       TO_CHAR(car_enters,   'HH24:MI:SS')             AS car_enters,\n       TO_CHAR(jonas_enters, 'HH24:MI:SS')             AS jonas_enters,\n       TO_CHAR(GREATEST(car_enters, jonas_enters), 'HH24:MI:SS') AS pick_up\nFROM   car\nJOIN   walker USING (zone_id)\nJOIN   zones z USING (zone_id)\nORDER  BY GREATEST(car_enters, jonas_enters);"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "### The entry as an event pattern\n\n**Question:** How would a running stream job detect the arrival of the car?\n\n**How the query answers it:** The same event, detected in the stream as a transition from *outside* to *inside*. `SDO_GEOM.RELATE` (a function) classifies each point of the car stream against the geofence `G-PK03`; `MATCH_RECOGNIZE` finds the first change. This is the pattern a running stream job would use to trigger the next step (M4).\n\n**Result:** the last point outside the geofence is at 12:51:20, the first inside at 12:51:30 – the same entry as in the join above. At the first point inside, the car is already standing (0 km/h); the stream ends with 6 points in the car park, until 12:52:20. The friend left Nuremberg at 12:08 – 43 minutes, the drive time the routing service calculated for the way back in 11f."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "WITH car_points AS (\n  SELECT c.event_ts,\n         c.speed_kmh,\n         SDO_GEOM.RELATE(c.geom, 'ANYINTERACT',\n                         (SELECT z.geom FROM zones z WHERE z.zone_id = 'G-PK03'),\n                         0.05) AS in_geofence\n  FROM   car_stream c\n)\nSELECT TO_CHAR(last_outside, 'HH24:MI:SS') AS last_point_outside,\n       TO_CHAR(entered,      'HH24:MI:SS') AS entered_geofence,\n       speed_at_entry_kmh,\n       points_inside\nFROM   car_points\nMATCH_RECOGNIZE (\n  ORDER BY event_ts\n  MEASURES LAST(outside.event_ts)  AS last_outside,\n           FIRST(inside.event_ts)  AS entered,\n           FIRST(inside.speed_kmh) AS speed_at_entry_kmh,\n           COUNT(inside.*)         AS points_inside\n  ONE ROW PER MATCH\n  PATTERN (outside+ inside+)\n  DEFINE outside AS outside.in_geofence = 'FALSE',\n         inside  AS inside.in_geofence  = 'TRUE'\n);"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 4. Route and drive time to the orthopaedics\n\nFrom the car park to the orthopaedics in Nuremberg.\n\n**Question:** How long is the drive, and which route does it take?\n\n**How the query answers it:**\n\n`SDO_GCDR.ELOC_ROUTE` calls the routing service of the Autonomous AI Database: route preference, units, start and destination (longitude before latitude) and vehicle type. The answer is JSON:\n\n- `$.routeResponse.route.time` and `.distance`, read with `JSON_VALUE … RETURNING NUMBER`\n- `$.routeResponse.route.geometry`, the route as a GeoJSON line; `SDO_UTIL.FROM_GEOJSON` turns it into an `SDO_GEOMETRY` that SQL Developer or Spatial Studio can draw on top of the OSM roads.\n\nFrom `PK03` to *Orthopädie am Stadtpark* (`PR10`).\n\n**Result:** 43 minutes and 64.5 km – the same values as in 11f, section 8. The route is a line (`SDO_GTYPE` 2002, SRID 4326) from the car park (11.4099, 49.7696) to the practice in Nuremberg (11.0986, 49.4439). As a geometry it can be drawn directly on the OSM road map; `ROUTE_POINTS` counts its vertices, so the notebook output stays readable without opening the geometry."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "WITH route AS (\n  SELECT /*+ MATERIALIZE */\n         pr.provider_id,\n         pr.name,\n         SDO_GCDR.ELOC_ROUTE('fastest', 'km', 'minute',\n                             (SELECT pk.geom.sdo_point.x FROM parking_lots pk WHERE pk.parking_id = 'PK03'), (SELECT pk.geom.sdo_point.y FROM parking_lots pk WHERE pk.parking_id = 'PK03'),\n                             pr.lon, pr.lat,\n                             'auto') AS route_json\n  FROM   providers pr\n  WHERE  pr.provider_id = 'PR10'\n)\nSELECT provider_id,\n       name,\n       ROUND(JSON_VALUE(route_json, '$.routeResponse.route.time'     RETURNING NUMBER))    AS drive_min,\n       ROUND(JSON_VALUE(route_json, '$.routeResponse.route.distance' RETURNING NUMBER), 1) AS drive_km,\n       SDO_UTIL.GETNUMVERTICES(\n         SDO_UTIL.FROM_GEOJSON(JSON_QUERY(route_json, '$.routeResponse.route.geometry' RETURNING CLOB)))   AS route_points,\n       SDO_UTIL.FROM_GEOJSON(JSON_QUERY(route_json, '$.routeResponse.route.geometry' RETURNING CLOB)) AS route_geom\nFROM   route;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "### Fastest, shortest, with traffic\n\n**Question:** How much does the route preference change the drive?\n\n**How the query answers it:** The same route with the three route preferences. The table value constructor `VALUES` provides one row per preference.\n\n**Result:** *fastest* and *traffic* both take 43 minutes for 64.5 km – no traffic delay on this route at the time of the query. *shortest* saves 14 km but takes 69 minutes, 26 minutes longer, on smaller roads. With a consultation at 14:00 and pick-up at 12:54, the fastest route arrives at about 13:37; the shortest would arrive at about 14:03 – too late. Values from the live service can change from run to run, especially with *traffic*."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "WITH preferences AS (\n  SELECT preference\n  FROM   (VALUES ('fastest'), ('shortest'), ('traffic')) v (preference)\n),\nroutes AS (\n  SELECT /*+ MATERIALIZE */\n         p.preference,\n         SDO_GCDR.ELOC_ROUTE(p.preference, 'km', 'minute',\n                             (SELECT pk.geom.sdo_point.x FROM parking_lots pk WHERE pk.parking_id = 'PK03'), (SELECT pk.geom.sdo_point.y FROM parking_lots pk WHERE pk.parking_id = 'PK03'),\n                             (SELECT pr.lon FROM providers pr WHERE pr.provider_id = 'PR10'),\n                             (SELECT pr.lat FROM providers pr WHERE pr.provider_id = 'PR10'),\n                             'auto') AS route_json\n  FROM   preferences p\n)\nSELECT preference,\n       ROUND(JSON_VALUE(route_json, '$.routeResponse.route.time'     RETURNING NUMBER))    AS drive_min,\n       ROUND(JSON_VALUE(route_json, '$.routeResponse.route.distance' RETURNING NUMBER), 1) AS drive_km\nFROM   routes\nORDER  BY drive_min, preference;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 5. Fallback for the talk: keep the routes in a table\n\nLive demos fail at the worst moment.\n\n**Question:** How do we keep the routes available if the routing service cannot be reached?\n\n**How the query answers it:** If the routing service or the conference Wi-Fi fails during the talk, the demo reads the routes from a table. `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS … AS SELECT` stores the answers once: from `PK03` to every practice in Nuremberg. Rerun after dropping the table to refresh it.\n\n**Result:** the routes to all nine practices in Nuremberg are stored, 41 to 46 minutes from the car park. *Orthopädie am Stadtpark* (PR10) has the same 43 minutes and 64.5 km as the live call in section 4. `CREATED_AT` shows when the answers were fetched – shown in Berlin time, because `SYSTIMESTAMP` returns the time zone of the database server (UTC in Autonomous Database). If the service fails during the talk, the demo continues from this table."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS route_cache AS\nSELECT pr.provider_id,\n       'fastest'                                                   AS preference,\n       SYSTIMESTAMP AT TIME ZONE 'Europe/Berlin'                   AS created_at,\n       SDO_GCDR.ELOC_ROUTE('fastest', 'km', 'minute',\n                           (SELECT pk.geom.sdo_point.x FROM parking_lots pk WHERE pk.parking_id = 'PK03'), (SELECT pk.geom.sdo_point.y FROM parking_lots pk WHERE pk.parking_id = 'PK03'),\n                           pr.lon, pr.lat, 'auto')                 AS route_json\nFROM   providers pr\nWHERE  pr.city = 'Nürnberg';"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: "SELECT r.provider_id,\n       p.name,\n       ROUND(JSON_VALUE(r.route_json, '$.routeResponse.route.time'     RETURNING NUMBER))    AS drive_min,\n       ROUND(JSON_VALUE(r.route_json, '$.routeResponse.route.distance' RETURNING NUMBER), 1) AS drive_km,\n       TO_CHAR(r.created_at AT TIME ZONE 'Europe/Berlin', 'YYYY-MM-DD HH24:MI') AS created_at\nFROM   route_cache r\nJOIN   providers p ON p.provider_id = r.provider_id\nORDER  BY drive_min, r.provider_id;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 6. Plan B from real map data: the nearest emergency departments\n\nWhat if the orthopaedics had no appointment today? Then Jonas needs an emergency department.\n\n**Question:** How many hospitals in the map data have an emergency department?\n\n**How the query answers it:** If the orthopaedics had no appointment today, Jonas would need an emergency department. The 37 hospitals in `OSM_HOSPITALS` come from OpenStreetMap; their OSM tags are stored as JSON text (`VARCHAR2` with `IS JSON` check). Only some of them have `emergency=yes`."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "SELECT COUNT(*)                                                         AS hospitals,\n       COUNT(CASE WHEN JSON_VALUE(tags, '$.emergency') = 'yes' THEN 1 END) AS with_emergency_department\nFROM   osm_hospitals;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "### Spatial operator and JSON filter in one query (pattern M3)\n\n**Question:** Which emergency departments are nearest to the car park, and how long is the drive?\n\n**How the query answers it:**\n\n`SDO_NN` with `sdo_batch_size` searches outward from the car park `PK03`, `JSON_VALUE(tags, '$.emergency') = 'yes'` keeps only hospitals with an emergency department. A second CTE adds the drive time for the three nearest.\n\n**Result:** the three nearest emergency departments, 25 to 38 km straight-line: *Klinik Hohe Warte* in Bayreuth (33 minutes), *Krankenhaus Martha-Maria* in Nuremberg (41 minutes) and *Universitätsklinikum Erlangen* (47 minutes). Erlangen is closer than Nuremberg as the crow flies, but the drive takes 6 minutes longer – straight-line distance finds the candidates, the road network ranks them. Erlangen has no city: the OSM object has no `addr:city` tag. Plan B, ready in one query."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "WITH nearest AS (\n  SELECT h.feature_id,\n         h.name,\n         JSON_VALUE(h.tags, '$.\"addr:city\"') AS city,\n         h.geom.sdo_point.x                  AS lon,\n         h.geom.sdo_point.y                  AS lat,\n         ROUND(SDO_NN_DISTANCE(1) / 1000, 1) AS straight_km\n  FROM   osm_hospitals h\n  WHERE  SDO_NN(h.geom, (SELECT pk.geom FROM parking_lots pk WHERE pk.parking_id = 'PK03'), 'sdo_batch_size=10 unit=M', 1) = 'TRUE'\n  AND    JSON_VALUE(h.tags, '$.emergency') = 'yes'\n  ORDER  BY SDO_NN_DISTANCE(1)\n  FETCH  FIRST 3 ROWS ONLY\n),\nroutes AS (\n  SELECT /*+ MATERIALIZE */\n         n.*,\n         SDO_GCDR.ELOC_ROUTE('fastest', 'km', 'minute',\n                             (SELECT pk.geom.sdo_point.x FROM parking_lots pk WHERE pk.parking_id = 'PK03'), (SELECT pk.geom.sdo_point.y FROM parking_lots pk WHERE pk.parking_id = 'PK03'),\n                             n.lon, n.lat, 'auto') AS route_json\n  FROM   nearest n\n)\nSELECT name,\n       city,\n       straight_km,\n       ROUND(JSON_VALUE(route_json, '$.routeResponse.route.time'     RETURNING NUMBER))    AS drive_min,\n       ROUND(JSON_VALUE(route_json, '$.routeResponse.route.distance' RETURNING NUMBER), 1) AS drive_km\nFROM   routes\nORDER  BY drive_min;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "### Summary\n\n- `SDO_NN` with a filter finds the nearest car park a car can reach: *Wanderparkplatz Hohe Leite*.\n- Two streams and a geofence give the pick-up time; `MATCH_RECOGNIZE` detects the entry as an event (M4).\n- `ELOC_ROUTE` returns drive time and route as JSON and GeoJSON; the answers are kept in `ROUTE_CACHE` as a fallback.\n- Plan B combines a spatial operator with a JSON filter on real OSM data (M3).\n- Next: **11h** – one query for everything."
    languageId: markdown
